1.reproduce_code:
// 1. Pull in the module under test
const CriticalRequestChains = require('lighthouse/lighthouse-core/gather/computed/critical-request-chains');

// 2. Mock up three records: a root page, an iframe document, and a child resource inside the iframe
const networkRecords = [];

// Root page (High priority, no initiator)
networkRecords.push({
  requestId: '0',
  _resourceType: { _category: 'document' },
  url: 'https://example.com/',
  finished: true,
  priority: () => 'High',
  initiatorRequest: () => null,
});

// Iframe document (still High priority, initiated by root)
networkRecords.push({
  requestId: '1',
  _resourceType: { _category: 'iframe' },
  url: 'https://youtube.com/embed/XYZ',
  finished: true,
  priority: () => 'High',
  initiatorRequest: () => networkRecords[0],
});

// A CSS file loaded inside that iframe
networkRecords.push({
  requestId: '2',
  _resourceType: { _category: 'stylesheet' },
  url: 'https://youtube.com/css/player.css',
  finished: true,
  priority: () => 'High',
  initiatorRequest: () => networkRecords[1],
});

// 3. Extract the critical chains
const chains = CriticalRequestChains.extractChain(networkRecords);

// 4. Print them out to confirm that the iframe node (1) and its child (2) appear
console.log('Critical Request Chains:', JSON.stringify(chains, null, 2));

1.explanation:
We create three fake network records: a top‐level HTML document (id ‘0’), an iframe document (id ‘1’) that was initiated by the root, and a stylesheet inside the iframe (id ‘2’). All three are assigned High priority. When you call CriticalRequestChains.extractChain(networkRecords) you’ll see output like:

{
  "0": { children: { "1": { children: { "2": { children: {} } } } } }
}

— showing that the iframe and its CSS child have been treated as critical requests. This reproduces the behavior seen in the PaulIrish.com screenshot, demonstrating the bug: iframes and their descendants are erroneously included in the CRC tree.
